The equation '
$$ L_L =  egin{pmatrix} 
u_e \ e \end{pmatrix}_L, \quad  egin{pmatrix} 
u_\mu \ \mu \end{pmatrix}_L, \quad  egin{pmatrix} 
u_T \ T \end{pmatrix}_L $$' defines three distinct lepton flavor doublets. Each doublet is a column vector representing the left-handed chiral states of a neutrino and its associated charged lepton. For instance, the first doublet '
$$  egin{pmatrix} 
u_e \ e \end{pmatrix}_L $$' comprises the left-handed electron neutrino (\( 
u_e \)_L) and the left-handed electron (\( e \)_L). Similarly, the second doublet contains the left-handed muon neutrino (\( 
u_\mu \)_L) and the left-handed muon (\( \mu \)_L). The third doublet consists of the left-handed tau neutrino (\( 
u_T \)_L) and the left-handed tau lepton (\( T \)_L). The subscript 'L' signifies that these are the left-handed components of the fermion fields. This grouping is crucial because the weak nuclear force interacts differently with left-handed and right-handed particles; specifically, the charged current weak interactions only couple to left-handed leptons and right-handed anti-leptons.
